suneung-2000|51|1|A Repair Workshop|Mina wanted to recruit three students for a bicycle repair workshop. At first, everyone wanted to paint the bikes, but nobody wanted to maintain the tools. Mina made a chart that gave each student both jobs. By Friday, the tools were clean and two bicycles were ready. The group decided to keep sharing the less popular work.|Mina가 작업표를 만든 이유는 무엇인가요?|인기 없는 도구 관리도 함께 맡도록 하려고|자전거를 모두 같은 색으로 칠하려고|수리할 자전거 수를 줄이려고|도구를 외부 회사에 맡기려고|Mina made a chart that gave each student both jobs.|학생마다 칠하기와 도구 관리 두 일을 맡겼으므로 덜 인기 있는 일도 나누려는 목적입니다.
suneung-2000|51|2|An Early Delivery|At dawn, Jun arrived to deliver a box of library books. A bulletin beside the door said that the entrance would remain locked until eight. Instead of leaving the box in the rain, he waited under the roof. The librarian thanked him for being patient. Every book was dry when the reading club opened its first meeting that morning.|Jun이 책 상자를 들고 기다린 까닭은 무엇인가요?|문이 잠겨 있어 책을 비 맞지 않게 지키려고|책 제목을 게시판에 모두 적으려고|독서 모임의 발표를 피하려고|사서가 책을 돌려달라고 해서|Instead of leaving the box in the rain, he waited under the roof.|문이 열릴 때까지 지붕 아래서 기다려 책을 비로부터 보호했습니다.
suneung-2000|52|1|Room for Another Idea|During a group discussion, one student sounded hostile when another proposed a different design. Their teacher asked them to show respect by repeating the other person's idea before answering it. This small rule did not force agreement. It helped them listen. By the end, they had combined two useful suggestions without letting disagreement discourage anyone from speaking again.|교사가 제시한 규칙의 핵심은 무엇인가요?|반박하기 전에 상대의 생각을 다시 말해 듣기|다수의 의견과 다른 말은 하지 않기|모든 제안을 그대로 채택하기|의견이 다른 학생끼리 따로 과제하기|Their teacher asked them to show respect by repeating the other person's idea before answering it.|상대의 의견을 먼저 되짚어 말하게 하여 존중하며 듣도록 했습니다.
suneung-2000|52|2|A Safer Step|The gardening club noticed a crack in a concrete step beside its shed. They wanted to fill it quickly, but the caretaker said paint was not a substitute for a proper repair. He closed that path and arranged for help. Students used the other entrance for a week. The bright new paint came only after the step was safe again.|수리가 끝나기 전에 학생들이 한 일은 무엇인가요?|다른 출입구를 이용했다|갈라진 부분에 페인트만 칠했다|정원 동아리 활동을 영구 중단했다|창고 전체를 새로 지었다|Students used the other entrance for a week.|갈라진 계단 쪽 길을 닫고 학생들은 일주일 동안 다른 출입구를 사용했습니다.
suneung-2000|53|1|Two Languages at the Museum|A small museum invited bilingual students to contribute to its welcome cards. Hana translated the opening hours, while Luis checked whether the directions were clear. Before printing, they asked the staff to notify them of any changes. Their goal was not to make the cards look impressive. They wanted visitors to find the right room without asking for help.|학생들이 안내 카드를 만든 주된 목적은 무엇인가요?|방문객이 필요한 장소를 쉽게 찾게 하려고|번역 실력을 서로 겨루려고|박물관의 방 이름을 새로 정하려고|개관 시간을 늘리려고|They wanted visitors to find the right room without asking for help.|방문객이 도움을 요청하지 않고 알맞은 방을 찾도록 돕는 것이 목적입니다.
suneung-2000|53|2|A Photograph from Another Era|Our town had a renowned photographer who recorded street life in an earlier era. When the library decided to convert his studio into a gallery, volunteers found several boxes without dates. They kept those pictures separate from the dated ones. Rather than guessing, they asked local residents for clues. The first exhibition included only photographs whose locations had been confirmed.|날짜가 없는 사진을 따로 둔 이유로 알맞은 것은?|확인되지 않은 정보를 함부로 단정하지 않으려고|오래된 사진을 모두 버리려고|전시 공간을 사진관으로 바꾸려고|주민들에게 사진을 판매하려고|Rather than guessing, they asked local residents for clues.|추측하는 대신 주민에게 단서를 요청하고 확인된 사진만 전시했습니다.
suneung-2000|54|1|A Peculiar Footprint|In a folktale read by our drama club, a peculiar footprint appears outside a village every winter. The villagers first blame a supernatural visitor. A child follows the marks and discovers that the baker's wooden shoe leaves them. Our club kept the mysterious opening but made the final scene funny. The audience learned the answer at the same moment as the villagers.|이야기에서 이상한 발자국을 남긴 것은 무엇인가요?|제빵사의 나무 신발|겨울마다 찾아오는 유령|마을 아이의 맨발|극단이 만든 무대 장치|A child follows the marks and discovers that the baker's wooden shoe leaves them.|아이가 자국을 따라가 나무 신발이 원인임을 알아냅니다.
suneung-2000|54|2|Waiting beside the Fountain|A gigantic model bird stood beside the fountain at an outdoor art show. Its moving wings tempted children to chase the shadows across the square. The artist noticed that some visitors could not see from their seats. She stopped the display and moved the chairs into a wider range of positions. Then everyone could watch without standing in the path.|예술가가 의자 위치를 바꾼 이유는 무엇인가요?|앉은 관람객들도 작품을 볼 수 있도록|새 모형의 날개 움직임을 멈추려고|분수에 들어갈 길을 만들려고|그림자를 더 빨리 움직이게 하려고|The artist noticed that some visitors could not see from their seats.|자리에서 볼 수 없는 관람객이 있어 의자를 여러 위치로 옮겼습니다.
suneung-2000|55|1|A Quiet Dormitory|At a summer camp, students in one dormitory agreed to conduct a small experiment. For three evenings, they put their phones in a basket before bedtime. One bold student predicted that nobody would enjoy it. Instead, they began telling stories and fell asleep earlier. They did not ban phones completely; they simply chose to repeat the quiet hour the following week.|실험 뒤 학생들이 내린 결정은 무엇인가요?|잠들기 전 조용한 시간을 다시 가져 보기로 했다|휴대전화를 캠프에서 영구 금지했다|실험을 첫날 바로 중단했다|이야기하는 시간을 밤새 늘렸다|They did not ban phones completely; they simply chose to repeat the quiet hour the following week.|휴대전화를 완전히 금지하지 않고 조용한 시간만 다음 주에 반복하기로 했습니다.
suneung-2000|55|2|A Change of Route|A troop of young hikers hoped to reach a low summit before lunch. At a shallow stream, their guide found that the wooden bridge was loose. She chose to alter the route even though the new path was longer. The group reached a different viewpoint and ate there. Their journal praised the clear view rather than treating the changed destination as a failure.|안내자가 길을 바꾼 직접적인 이유는 무엇인가요?|개울의 나무다리가 흔들렸기 때문에|점심 도시락을 두고 왔기 때문에|다른 무리를 추월하려고|정상에 가는 길을 잊었기 때문에|At a shallow stream, their guide found that the wooden bridge was loose.|안전하지 않은 다리를 발견한 것이 경로 변경의 직접적인 이유입니다.
suneung-2000|56|1|A Banner That Helped|A neighborhood clinic held a free walking event. Its first banner listed many health terms, and few people stopped to read it. Volunteers replaced it with a simple invitation and a clear meeting time. They also offered a map as an incentive to join. More neighbors came the next weekend, including people who had never joined a group walk before.|두 번째 안내에서 달라진 점은 무엇인가요?|초대 문구와 모임 시간을 쉽게 제시했다|건강 전문 용어를 더 많이 넣었다|참가비를 새로 받기 시작했다|지도를 참가자에게서 거두었다|Volunteers replaced it with a simple invitation and a clear meeting time.|어려운 용어 대신 쉬운 초대와 명확한 시간을 제시했습니다.
suneung-2000|56|2|The Mark on the Costume|Lea borrowed a costume for a school heritage festival and found a small stain on its sleeve. She was tempted to conceal it under a ribbon. Instead, she told the costume keeper before trying to clean it. He knew which method was safe for the cloth. Lea later received warm applause for her performance, but she was especially glad she had asked first.|Lea가 직접 얼룩을 지우기 전에 한 일은 무엇인가요?|의상 관리인에게 알렸다|리본으로 얼룩을 숨겼다|공연을 취소했다|다른 의상을 몰래 가져갔다|Instead, she told the costume keeper before trying to clean it.|얼룩을 숨기거나 임의로 처리하지 않고 먼저 관리인에게 알렸습니다.
suneung-2000|57|1|The First Draft|Our club's first draft of a festival plan put every activity in one hour. It looked exciting, but nobody could manage all the jobs at once. We asked another club how to adapt the schedule. Their advice was to leave short breaks between events. After we changed the plan, helpers had time to move equipment and visitors no longer missed the next activity.|수정된 계획이 더 나았던 이유는 무엇인가요?|행사 사이에 이동하고 준비할 시간이 생겨서|모든 활동을 같은 시각에 시작해서|다른 동아리의 참가를 막아서|축제에서 장비를 전혀 쓰지 않아서|After we changed the plan, helpers had time to move equipment and visitors no longer missed the next activity.|활동 사이의 여유가 장비 이동과 다음 활동 참여를 가능하게 했습니다.
suneung-2000|57|2|The Shared Garden|A low rope marked the boundary of our shared garden. We wanted a spacious path, so we moved the flower pots closer together rather than taking our neighbor's land. One student offered to weave labels from leftover strips of cloth. When the path opened, its improved access was a tangible result that everyone could see, including visitors using walking frames.|동아리가 통로를 넓히기 위해 한 일은 무엇인가요?|화분 사이의 간격을 줄였다|이웃의 땅을 가져왔다|정원 출입을 전부 금지했다|표지판을 없애 버렸다|We wanted a spacious path, so we moved the flower pots closer together rather than taking our neighbor's land.|다른 땅을 침범하지 않고 화분 배치를 바꾸어 공간을 확보했습니다.
suneung-2000|58|1|One Shelf at a Time|Tara had an ambitious plan to build a library in an empty classroom. Books began to accumulate faster than she could sort them. She did her utmost to finish alone, then realized that effort needed a system. She asked friends to label one shelf each. Within two afternoons, the room was useful, even though some donated books still needed checking.|Tara가 일을 해결하는 데 도움이 된 것은 무엇인가요?|친구들에게 선반별로 정리 일을 나눈 것|책을 받는 일을 영구 중단한 것|모든 책을 혼자 동시에 읽은 것|분류하지 않은 책을 모두 버린 것|She asked friends to label one shelf each.|혼자 하던 일을 선반별로 나눈 뒤 교실을 쓸 수 있게 되었습니다.
suneung-2000|58|2|The Delivery Map|Students who would attend the science fair needed a way to navigate a large school. Before they could dispatch the invitations, the organizers tested their map with two new visitors. Both visitors missed the lift. The team decided to modify the map by adding a clear symbol beside it. The next testers found the accessible route without any spoken directions.|시험 방문객의 경험으로 지도에서 무엇을 바꾸었나요?|승강기 위치에 명확한 기호를 추가했다|초대장을 보내지 않기로 했다|과학전 장소를 다른 학교로 옮겼다|접근 가능한 길을 지도에서 지웠다|The team decided to modify the map by adding a clear symbol beside it.|승강기를 놓쳤다는 관찰을 바탕으로 해당 위치의 표시를 보강했습니다.
suneung-2000|59|1|The Stone Exhibit|At a geology exhibit, one display invited visitors to compare the diameter of several round stones. Nuri measured the widest part carefully, then noticed that her first reading included the stand beneath a stone. She measured again and corrected her card. The guide praised the correction. The activity was meant to teach careful observation, not reward whoever finished the measurements first.|Nuri가 측정값을 수정한 이유는 무엇인가요?|처음에는 돌받침까지 함께 측정해서|돌이 측정 중에 커져서|다른 관람객의 기록을 베끼려고|가장 빨리 끝내야 상품을 받아서|Nuri measured the widest part carefully, then noticed that her first reading included the stand beneath a stone.|돌의 지름만 재야 하는데 받침이 포함된 것을 발견해 다시 측정했습니다.
suneung-2000|59|2|A Story behind the Price|For a class market, Jae wrote an anecdote about making his cloth bookmarks. A visitor enjoyed the story but asked why one bookmark cost more than another. Jae explained that the larger one used twice as much fabric. He hoped to earn enough to buy more thread, yet he chose to display the material costs so buyers could understand the difference.|Jae가 재료비를 표시한 이유는 무엇인가요?|책갈피의 가격 차이를 구매자가 이해하도록|방문객에게 실을 직접 사 오게 하려고|큰 책갈피가 더 싸다고 주장하려고|모든 물건을 무료로 나누어 주려고|He hoped to earn enough to buy more thread, yet he chose to display the material costs so buyers could understand the difference.|재료비를 공개해 책갈피마다 가격이 다른 까닭을 설명했습니다.
suneung-2000|60|1|Changing the Script|Dani and her sibling wrote a short script about a lost dog. When their friends read it, nobody understood why the final character suddenly left. They met to discuss the missing information instead of adding a long speech. A brief scene showing an urgent phone call solved the problem. The ending stayed the same, but the audience could now follow the character's decision.|대본의 문제를 해결하기 위해 추가한 것은 무엇인가요?|긴급한 전화가 오는 짧은 장면|줄거리와 관련 없는 긴 연설|전혀 다른 결말|마지막 인물을 소개하는 광고|A brief scene showing an urgent phone call solved the problem.|갑자기 떠나는 이유를 알려 주는 전화 장면을 넣어 행동을 이해하게 했습니다.
suneung-2000|60|2|The Garden Reminder|A painted pillar at the school garden held a weekly task board. Its purpose was to remind volunteers when to feed the rabbits and where to lay the clean tools. At first, only one person could read the tiny writing. The club replaced it with large labels and simple pictures. After that, younger helpers could complete the same jobs without waiting for an older student.|게시판을 바꾼 결과는 무엇인가요?|어린 도우미들도 혼자 할 일을 알아볼 수 있었다|토끼에게 먹이를 주는 일이 없어졌다|연장 보관 장소를 폐쇄했다|나이 든 학생만 정원에 들어올 수 있었다|After that, younger helpers could complete the same jobs without waiting for an older student.|큰 글씨와 그림 덕분에 어린 학생도 지시를 이해하고 일을 수행했습니다.
suneung-2000|61|1|Before the Expedition|Before a class expedition to a swamp, our teacher checked the storage boxes twice. One contained notebooks but no waterproof bags. Rather than hope for dry weather, she asked us to pack the missing bags. Rain began during our walk. We returned with wet shoes and dry notes, which let us compare our observations in the classroom the next day.|미리 챙긴 방수 가방이 도움이 된 이유는 무엇인가요?|비가 와도 관찰 기록이 젖지 않았기 때문에|습지에서 신발을 전혀 신지 않아도 돼서|걸어갈 거리를 줄일 수 있어서|노트를 교실에 두고 가도 돼서|We returned with wet shoes and dry notes, which let us compare our observations in the classroom the next day.|신발은 젖었지만 노트는 마른 채로 남아 다음 날 관찰 내용을 비교할 수 있었습니다.
suneung-2000|61|2|Checking the Numbers|In a story set near the equator, a student records the cost of fruit at a market. Our arithmetic group used those fictional prices to practice making a budget. We kept the units consistent instead of mixing kilograms and individual pieces. When one total looked strange, we checked its units first. The mistake came from comparing two different kinds of prices.|그룹이 이상한 합계를 확인할 때 먼저 살펴본 것은?|가격에 사용한 단위|이야기 속 학생의 나이|시장의 실제 위치|과일 껍질의 색|When one total looked strange, we checked its units first.|가격 단위가 서로 다르면 비교가 어긋날 수 있어 먼저 단위를 확인했습니다.
suneung-2000|62|1|Preparing for Visitors|For an upcoming open day, the art club needed to allocate tables to several groups. Everyone wanted a place near the entrance. Rather than rush into a decision, the organizers asked which activities needed water or electricity. They matched those needs to available spaces. A quiet drawing group chose a corner table and later said it was easier to talk to visitors there.|주최 측이 탁자 위치를 정할 때 사용한 기준은 무엇인가요?|활동에 물이나 전기가 필요한지|가장 먼저 요구한 사람이 누구인지|탁자의 색이 가장 밝은지|입구에서 무조건 가장 가까운지|Rather than rush into a decision, the organizers asked which activities needed water or electricity.|공간 배치는 단순한 자리 경쟁이 아니라 활동의 실제 필요를 기준으로 했습니다.
suneung-2000|62|2|Finding the Source|A school hygiene team wanted to locate the source of muddy water beside a drinking tap. Some students blamed an external pipe. The team did not open any pipes themselves; they reported what they saw to the caretaker. He found a leaking flower pot above the drain. It did not contaminate the drinking water, but it made the floor slippery.|탁한 물이 생긴 원인으로 밝혀진 것은 무엇인가요?|배수구 위 화분에서 새는 물|음수대의 외부 수도관|학생들이 연 수도관|오염된 마실 물|He found a leaking flower pot above the drain.|관리인이 배수구 위 화분의 누수를 발견했으며 음용수 오염은 아니었습니다.
suneung-2000|63|1|A Shape in the Soil|Students went to explore the old garden before planting new trees. A curved ceramic piece began to emerge from the soil. They stopped digging, marked the spot and told their teacher. Nobody claimed it was an ancient treasure. Later, the gardener identified it as part of a broken flower pot. The class still enjoyed describing how they had investigated their find.|발견 직후 학생들이 보인 태도로 알맞은 것은?|판단을 서두르지 않고 교사에게 알렸다|고대 보물이라고 바로 발표했다|도자기 조각을 몰래 팔았다|정원의 모든 흙을 즉시 파냈다|They stopped digging, marked the spot and told their teacher.|발견 장소를 표시하고 교사에게 알려 성급하게 정체를 단정하지 않았습니다.
suneung-2000|63|2|A Small Mistake|Bo accidentally erased a friend's drawing from a shared tablet. He first called the mistake trivial, but his friend had spent an hour on it. Bo decided to confess exactly what he had done and acknowledge the lost effort. They found an earlier saved copy together. Their reconciliation began with an honest apology rather than an excuse about the size of the mistake.|두 친구가 화해를 시작할 수 있었던 계기는 무엇인가요?|Bo가 잘못과 친구의 노력을 인정한 것|지워진 그림이 중요하지 않다고 우긴 것|친구에게 저장 책임을 모두 돌린 것|이전 그림 파일을 숨긴 것|Their reconciliation began with an honest apology rather than an excuse about the size of the mistake.|잘못을 축소하는 변명 대신 진심으로 사과하며 화해가 시작되었습니다.
suneung-2000|64|1|More than a Certificate|A swimming coach gave every beginner a certificate, but she wanted it to convey more than praise. Each card listed one skill learned and one safe next step. Students who could float were reminded to practice in shallow water with an adult. Greater depth was not presented as a prize. The cards showed personal progress without encouraging anyone to take unnecessary risks.|수료증에 배운 기술과 다음 단계를 적은 목적은 무엇인가요?|안전하게 자신의 발전을 이해하도록|더 깊은 물에 혼자 가도록|학생들의 순위를 발표하도록|모든 학생의 실력이 같다고 알리도록|The cards showed personal progress without encouraging anyone to take unnecessary risks.|불필요한 위험을 부추기지 않으면서 개인별 진전을 보여 주는 것이 목적입니다.
suneung-2000|64|2|After the Picnic|After a picnic, our class sorted the leftover food before cleaning the tables. A sour smell came from a closed container, so nobody tasted its contents. An adult handled the food and put away the sharp blade used for fruit. We wiped the tables and left them open to the air so the remaining water could evaporate before the room was locked.|신 냄새가 나는 통을 발견했을 때 학생들은 어떻게 했나요?|내용물을 맛보지 않고 어른에게 맡겼다|모두 한 입씩 맛보았다|칼로 통을 잘라 열었다|남은 음식을 전부 그 통에 섞었다|A sour smell came from a closed container, so nobody tasted its contents.|냄새가 나는 내용물을 직접 맛보지 않았고 이후 어른이 처리했습니다.
suneung-2000|65|1|The Small Cafe|We wanted our school cafe corner to feel cozy rather than crowded. At first, the menu offered ten kinds of beverage. A volunteer suggested keeping only three and posting their ingredients clearly. Visitors could choose quickly and ask about anything unfamiliar. When we asked what they would recommend for the next event, most people said to keep the clear menu and comfortable seats.|방문객들이 다음 행사에도 유지하자고 한 것은 무엇인가요?|알기 쉬운 메뉴와 편안한 좌석|더 많은 음료 종류와 빽빽한 좌석|성분을 숨긴 긴 메뉴|음료를 고를 시간을 제한하는 규칙|When we asked what they would recommend for the next event, most people said to keep the clear menu and comfortable seats.|방문객의 추천 내용이 마지막 문장에 직접 제시됩니다.
suneung-2000|65|2|An Auction for the Club|Our club had an abundant supply of donated puzzles, including several duplicate copies. We organized a small auction to raise money for new shelves. Before selling anything, we counted the pieces and labeled incomplete boxes honestly. We also arranged a free table to accommodate visitors who did not want to bid. By afternoon, both the auction and the free table had found many users.|동아리가 무료 나눔 탁자도 마련한 이유는 무엇인가요?|경매에 참여하지 않는 방문객도 배려하려고|퍼즐의 빠진 조각을 숨기려고|책장 모금을 취소하려고|모든 방문객에게 입찰을 강요하려고|We also arranged a free table to accommodate visitors who did not want to bid.|입찰을 원하지 않는 사람도 행사에 참여할 수 있도록 한 배려입니다.
suneung-2000|66|1|Listening after the Concert|After a concert in the auditorium, our band asked for feedback on small cards. We expected comments about the songs, but several listeners mentioned that spoken announcements were too quiet. We decided to retain the song order and improve the microphone checks. At the next rehearsal, one member sat at the back and tested whether every announcement could be understood from there.|밴드가 받은 의견을 바탕으로 바꾼 것은 무엇인가요?|안내 방송을 잘 들을 수 있도록 점검을 강화했다|노래 순서를 전부 뒤집었다|공연 뒤 의견을 받지 않기로 했다|객석 뒤쪽 자리를 없앴다|We decided to retain the song order and improve the microphone checks.|곡 순서는 유지하고 마이크 점검을 개선하기로 했습니다.
suneung-2000|66|2|The Dusty Model|The technology club displayed a model of farm machinery. After several weeks, a buildup of dust made its moving parts hard to see. We realized we had begun to neglect the display after finishing it. Cleaning helped, but we also wrote a weekly care list. We could not undo the delay, yet we could stop the same problem from returning so soon.|문제가 빨리 재발하지 않도록 동아리가 한 일은 무엇인가요?|주간 관리 목록을 작성했다|모형의 모든 움직이는 부분을 버렸다|먼지가 없었다고 주장했다|전시를 다시는 만들지 않기로 했다|Cleaning helped, but we also wrote a weekly care list.|한 번 청소하는 데 그치지 않고 정기적으로 관리할 계획을 세웠습니다.
suneung-2000|67|1|A Garden in the City|For an ecology project, our class observed a tiny garden in a metropolitan neighborhood. A fence helped shield the plants from footballs, but its solid panels also hid the flowers from younger children. We proposed a lower section near the path. The gardener agreed to test it in one place first, so the class could watch whether protection and visibility could improve together.|낮은 울타리를 한 구간에서 먼저 시험한 이유는 무엇인가요?|식물 보호와 관람이 함께 나아지는지 살펴보려고|모든 꽃을 다른 동네로 옮기려고|축구 경기를 정원 안에서 열려고|어린이의 관찰을 완전히 막으려고|The gardener agreed to test it in one place first, so the class could watch whether protection and visibility could improve together.|보호 기능과 잘 보이는 정도를 함께 확인하기 위한 작은 시험입니다.
suneung-2000|67|2|The Newspaper Drawing|A student drew the interior of a pretend courtroom for our school newspaper. A visiting attorney noticed that the picture left no room for people watching the case. The editor added seats in the margin and checked the labels. The article's coverage was still about a fictional trial, but the corrected illustration helped readers understand who would be in the room and where they might sit.|변호사의 지적을 듣고 삽화에 추가한 것은 무엇인가요?|재판을 지켜보는 사람들의 좌석|법정 밖의 주차장|기사에 없는 실제 사건 이름|신문의 판매 가격|The editor added seats in the margin and checked the labels.|관람하는 사람의 공간이 없다는 지적을 받아 좌석을 덧붙이고 표시를 점검했습니다.
suneung-2000|68|1|The Stage Curtain|Our drama club built a light wooden foundation for a small model stage. When the table began to vibrate, the fabric curtain kept falling down. We first tried heavier cloth, which made the model harder to carry. Then we fastened the curtain rod more carefully. The same light curtain stayed in place, and the group learned to check the support before replacing the material.|커튼이 떨어지는 문제를 실제로 해결한 것은 무엇인가요?|커튼 봉을 더 단단히 고정한 것|천을 계속 더 무겁게 바꾼 것|무대를 다른 방에 숨긴 것|모든 커튼을 없애 버린 것|Then we fastened the curtain rod more carefully.|가벼운 천을 그대로 두고 지지대를 고친 뒤 커튼이 제자리에 유지되었습니다.
suneung-2000|68|2|Sharing the Room|Two clubs had an urgent need for the same practice room before a festival. Their conflict grew when both posted full schedules on the door. A teacher suggested a framework with short shared periods and separate quiet work. The students used it to settle the immediate problem. They also agreed to book future dates together, before either club announced them to its members.|두 동아리가 앞으로 같은 문제를 줄이기 위해 약속한 것은?|각자 발표하기 전에 함께 날짜를 예약하기|한 동아리만 방을 영구 독점하기|축제를 해마다 취소하기|연습 시간표를 아예 만들지 않기|They also agreed to book future dates together, before either club announced them to its members.|향후 일정은 일방적으로 알리기 전에 공동으로 정하기로 했습니다.
suneung-2000|69|1|The Unsigned Note|Our literature circle found an anonymous note inside a donated novel. It contained a short quote from the book and a question about the main character. We did not try to identify the writer. Instead, we placed a blank card beside it and invited another reader to respond. Soon the display held several different interpretations, all connected to words that readers could find in the story.|독서 모임이 이름 없는 쪽지를 활용한 방법은 무엇인가요?|다른 독자가 답을 적도록 카드를 마련했다|글쓴이의 신원을 공개적으로 찾았다|책 속 인용문을 지워 버렸다|해석이 하나뿐이라고 공지했다|Instead, we placed a blank card beside it and invited another reader to respond.|작성자를 추적하는 대신 독자들이 생각을 이어 쓰는 계기로 삼았습니다.
suneung-2000|69|2|The Floating Model|During a technical project, our group built a model dock for a tray of water. A small anchor was supposed to help it withstand waves from a hand fan. The first anchor slid across the tray. Before we could implement a new design, we recorded what had moved. We then changed only the anchor's shape, keeping the fan speed and dock the same.|두 번째 실험에서 다른 조건을 같게 둔 이유로 가장 알맞은 것은?|닻 모양을 바꾼 효과를 확인하려고|선풍기 속도가 기록에 필요 없어서|첫 실험의 실패를 숨기려고|부두 모형을 만들지 않았기 때문에|We then changed only the anchor's shape, keeping the fan speed and dock the same.|다른 조건을 유지하고 한 요소만 바꾸어 닻 모양의 영향을 살펴봅니다.
suneung-2000|70|1|The Orchard Sketch|A solitary tree stood at the edge of an old orchard. In my first sketch, it seemed to resemble a giant umbrella. My partner saw a dancer instead. We returned to the same place and drew it from two different angles. The shapes looked different again. Neither sketch had to defeat the other; each recorded a particular way of looking at the tree.|두 사람의 스케치 경험이 보여 주는 것은 무엇인가요?|같은 대상도 보는 각도에 따라 다르게 보일 수 있다|나무 그림에는 언제나 정답 하나가 있다|혼자 그린 그림은 가치가 없다|다른 의견은 반드시 하나로 통일해야 한다|We returned to the same place and drew it from two different angles.|같은 나무를 다른 각도에서 보자 모양이 다시 달라져 관점의 차이를 보여 줍니다.
suneung-2000|70|2|A Clear Signal|Our gymnastics club invited a student referee to help with a friendly team event. Before it began, she explained the sequence of turns and showed a flag for stopping. When a loose mat threatened to disrupt a routine, she raised the flag immediately. The team waited while an adult fixed it. Everyone then restarted from the beginning rather than guessing where to continue.|매트가 헐거워졌을 때 심판이 한 행동은 무엇인가요?|깃발을 들어 즉시 멈추게 했다|순서를 무시하고 경기를 계속했다|학생들에게 직접 위험한 수리를 시켰다|이미 끝난 것으로 처리했다|When a loose mat threatened to disrupt a routine, she raised the flag immediately.|중단 신호를 즉시 보내고 어른이 매트를 고칠 때까지 기다렸습니다.
suneung-2000|71|1|Testing the Robot|Our club's robot was meant to carry a toy vacuum across a model room. We used blocks to simulate furniture and wrote a hypothesis that a wider turning space would prevent collisions. In the first trial, we also changed the wheel speed. That made the result hard to interpret. For the next trial, we kept the speed constant and changed only the turning space.|첫 실험 결과를 해석하기 어려웠던 이유는 무엇인가요?|회전 공간과 바퀴 속도를 함께 바꾸었기 때문에|모형 가구를 전혀 배치하지 않아서|가설을 실험 뒤에만 읽어서|청소기를 실제 방에서 잃어버려서|In the first trial, we also changed the wheel speed.|공간뿐 아니라 속도도 달라져 어떤 변화가 충돌에 영향을 주었는지 구분하기 어렵습니다.
suneung-2000|71|2|The Paper City|For a class spectacle, we built a paper city with one skyscraper and a tiny fortress. Reed stems supported a bridge between them. The bridge bent when we added too many paper figures, so we removed some and added another support. Our city was imaginary, but the model still had to stand. Visitors enjoyed the little story explaining how its bridge had been repaired.|모형 도시의 다리를 고친 방법은 무엇인가요?|인형 수를 줄이고 지지대를 보강했다|빌딩을 더 높이만 쌓았다|다리 위에 인형을 더 올렸다|방문객에게 다리를 들고 있게 했다|The bridge bent when we added too many paper figures, so we removed some and added another support.|무게를 줄이고 받치는 부분을 보강해 휘어지는 문제를 해결했습니다.
suneung-2000|72|1|Dividing the Work|We tried to divide a long mural into sections for four painters. On the first morning, two sections began to overlap, and rising fatigue made the conversation tense. We stopped to mark the shared edges clearly. Then we agreed that only one person would finish each border. The colors remained varied, but nobody had to paint over another student's work again.|벽화 작업에서 역할을 더 분명히 한 부분은 어디인가요?|구역이 맞닿는 경계 부분|그림을 전시할 날짜|붓을 구입할 가게|그림에 사용할 모든 색|Then we agreed that only one person would finish each border.|서로 겹쳐 칠하던 경계는 한 사람만 마무리하도록 정했습니다.
suneung-2000|72|2|The Orange Peel|An odd noise interrupted our cooking club's quiet activity. A nervous beginner had dropped an orange peel and jumped back from it. Our leader laughed at her own first cooking mistake to ease the tension, then asked everyone to clean their work areas. The student stayed and finished the recipe. Having a small job to do helped her overcome the embarrassment without becoming the center of attention.|초보 학생이 당황스러움을 이기는 데 도움이 된 것은?|작은 정리 일을 맡고 활동을 계속한 것|모든 사람 앞에서 실수를 자세히 발표한 것|요리 수업에서 바로 나간 것|다른 학생의 실수를 찾아낸 것|Having a small job to do helped her overcome the embarrassment without becoming the center of attention.|주목을 강요하지 않고 작은 일로 활동에 다시 참여하게 한 것이 도움이 되었습니다.
suneung-2000|73|1|A Vertical Test|For a classroom experiment, we dropped paper shapes beside a vertical ruler. Each measurement was supposed to begin from the same height, but one student held the shapes higher. We marked a starting line and repeated the trials. The second set of results was easier to compare. The group wrote down the change so that another class could repeat the same procedure accurately.|실험에서 출발선을 표시한 목적은 무엇인가요?|모든 종이 모양을 같은 높이에서 떨어뜨리려고|더 복잡한 모양만 선택하려고|결과를 기록하지 않으려고|자의 세로 길이를 늘리려고|We marked a starting line and repeated the trials.|놓는 높이를 통일해 각 측정 결과를 제대로 비교하도록 했습니다.
suneung-2000|73|2|Packing the Costumes|Our drama club needed to send a bundle of costumes to another school. Each garment had a different owner, so we refused to put loose name stickers directly on the cloth. Instead, we would enclose each item in a clean bag and insert a card with its owner's name. A checklist stayed outside the bundle, allowing the receiving teacher to check everything without guessing.|이름 카드를 옷에 직접 붙이지 않고 가방 안에 넣은 이유는?|옷을 보호하면서 주인을 구별하려고|받는 학교가 주인을 모르게 하려고|모든 의상을 같은 사람의 것으로 바꾸려고|의상 목록을 만들지 않으려고|Instead, we would enclose each item in a clean bag and insert a card with its owner's name.|옷에 직접 접착물을 붙이지 않으면서 각 소유자를 식별할 수 있게 했습니다.
suneung-2000|74|1|Seeing the Scene|My writing mentor said that a vivid scene did not need a long list of adjectives. She asked me to describe what one character noticed while entering a room. I wrote about a cold cup and an unopened letter. My partner could now comprehend why the character felt uneasy, even though I had removed the sentence that directly named the feeling.|글을 고친 뒤 독자가 인물의 감정을 이해한 단서는 무엇인가요?|식은 컵과 뜯지 않은 편지 같은 구체적인 사물|새로 추가한 긴 형용사 목록|인물의 감정을 직접 설명한 문장|이야기와 관계없는 장소 이름|I wrote about a cold cup and an unopened letter.|구체적인 장면의 단서로 불안한 감정을 전달하여 직접 감정명을 쓰지 않아도 이해하게 했습니다.
suneung-2000|74|2|Lunch after the Walk|After a long walk, our class had a strong appetite. An adult used the grill while students set the table and sliced bread with supervision. One loaf had a very hard crust, so we served it in thinner pieces. Everyone could choose what was comfortable to eat. We came to appreciate the extra care that made a simple shared lunch enjoyable for different people.|딱딱한 빵을 더 얇게 썬 이유로 알맞은 것은?|사람들이 편하게 먹을 수 있도록|빵을 전부 버리려고|점심 시간을 없애려고|학생들이 불을 직접 다루게 하려고|One loaf had a very hard crust, so we served it in thinner pieces.|딱딱한 겉부분이 있는 빵도 편하게 먹도록 크기를 조정했습니다.
suneung-2000|75|1|Two Ways to Join|An outgoing student volunteered to lead our academic discussion group. She preferred quick verbal answers, but noticed that one classmate had good ideas only after a pause. For the next meeting, she offered a minute of silent note writing before anyone spoke. The quieter student then shared a useful question. The group kept both writing and speaking as ways to join the discussion.|다음 모임에서 조용히 메모할 시간을 준 이유는 무엇인가요?|생각을 정리할 시간이 필요한 학생도 참여하도록|말하기 활동을 완전히 없애려고|빨리 답하는 학생에게만 점수를 주려고|토론 주제를 숨기려고|For the next meeting, she offered a minute of silent note writing before anyone spoke.|말하기 전에 생각을 정리할 시간을 마련하여 참여 방식의 폭을 넓혔습니다.
suneung-2000|75|2|The Garden Shelter|Our garden shed became a refuge from a sudden shower during an irrigation project. Some students felt drowsy after carrying heavy watering cans, so we paused instead of rushing to finish. Our teacher called the project a shared endeavor, not a race. When the rain stopped, we checked the paths and resumed the work in smaller groups with lighter loads of water.|비가 그친 뒤 학생들이 일을 재개한 방식은 무엇인가요?|작은 모둠으로 나누고 물의 무게를 줄였다|더 무거운 물통을 혼자 들었다|젖은 길을 확인하지 않고 달렸다|모든 일을 한 사람에게 맡겼다|When the rain stopped, we checked the paths and resumed the work in smaller groups with lighter loads of water.|길을 확인한 뒤 작은 모둠과 가벼운 짐으로 부담을 낮춰 작업을 이어갔습니다.
